@node Overview
@section Overview
@cindex agent loop

When you run Codebud with a natural-language request, it goes through
four stages:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Plan} --- The LLM reads your request and the list of available
tools.  It produces a JSON plan: an ordered list of steps, each
specifying a tool name, a description, and the arguments to pass to
that tool.

@item
@strong{Validate} --- The safety engine inspects every step in the plan.
Steps that would run commands not listed in the safety policy are
rejected before anything executes.

@item
@strong{Execute} --- The executor runs each step in order, collecting
the output (standard output, standard error, return code, or file
contents).

@item
@strong{Remember} --- The memory store records what happened so that
future requests in the same session can reference it.
@end enumerate

The @command{run} subcommand performs all four stages automatically.
The @command{plan} subcommand stops after stage 2 so you can inspect
the plan before committing to execution.  The @command{chat} subcommand
repeats all four stages across multiple turns using a single persistent
agent instance.

@strong{Timing:} On CPU-only hardware (for example, an Intel
i7-8550U laptop), planning with the recommended 3B model takes about
30 seconds for the first request of a session and about 8 seconds for
later ones, once Ollama has cached the start of the prompt.  This is
normal.  Codebud shows a live
progress indicator so you can see that it is working.
(@pxref{Progress Output}).


@node Quick Start
@section Quick Start
@cindex quick start

Before running Codebud, make sure the Ollama server is running and the
model is downloaded.  If you are unsure, run:

@example
codebud doctor
@end example

If all checks pass, send your first request:

@example
OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M \
    codebud run "list the Python files in the current directory"
@end example

You will see a spinner while the model thinks, then a numbered plan, then
the results of each step.  That is all there is to a basic run.

To avoid typing the model name every time, export it in your shell:

@example
export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
codebud run "list the Python files in the current directory"
@end example

Add the @code{export} line to @file{~/.zshrc} (or @file{~/.bashrc}) to
make it permanent.


@node Subcommands
@section Subcommands
@cindex subcommands

Codebud organises its functionality into subcommands:

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .75
@headitem Subcommand @tab Purpose
@item @command{run} @tab Plan and auto-execute a request (most common)
@item @command{plan} @tab Plan only --- inspect without executing
@item @command{chat} @tab Interactive multi-turn session
@item @command{doctor} @tab Check the environment
@item @command{history} @tab Show past step executions
@item @command{sessions} @tab List saved chat sessions
@item @command{index} @tab Build or refresh the project index for search
@item @command{skill} @tab Show or install Codebud's skill for other agents
@item @command{models} @tab List downloaded Ollama models
@item @command{config} @tab Show resolved configuration
@item @command{profiles} @tab List the profiles
@item @command{trust} @tab Review, trust or stop trusting the project's command policy
@item @command{key} @tab Create or manage the encryption key for data at rest
@item @command{version} @tab Show version information
@end multitable

All subcommands accept @option{--help} for a quick reminder:

@example
codebud run --help
@end example


@node The run Subcommand
@section The @command{run} Subcommand
@cindex run subcommand
@cindex executing a plan

@command{run} is the main way to use Codebud.  It sends your request to
the LLM, receives a plan, and executes every step automatically.

@example
codebud run "@var{your request}"
@end example

Example:

@example
codebud run "create a file called hello.py that prints Hello, world"
@end example

Codebud will:

@enumerate
@item
Show a live spinner while the model generates the plan.
@item
Print a numbered plan summary.
@item
Execute each step in sequence, printing its full standard output
(never truncated --- @pxref{Reading Step Results}).
@end enumerate

If any step fails, Codebud stops, prints the error, and suggests a fix.

Powerful commands --- code runners such as @code{python} and @code{pytest},
file-changing commands such as @code{cp} (@pxref{Powerful Commands}) ---
are not run blindly: @command{run} stops before each one and asks
@samp{Run it? [y/N]}.  So do web searches, which send their query to
DuckDuckGo (the query is shown).  If nobody can answer (standard input is
closed, as in a script or cron job), the step is not run and @command{run}
exits with status 1, telling you to use @option{--yes}.

@table @option
@item --yes / -y
Run powerful commands and web searches without asking.  Use it in
scripts once you trust the request:

@example
codebud run --yes "run the test suite"
@end example

The allow-list and refused arguments still apply (@pxref{The Safety Engine}).

@item --interactive / -i
Prompt for approval before executing each step, instead of running the
whole plan automatically:

@example
codebud run -i "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

At each step you can answer @kbd{y} (execute), @kbd{n} or @key{RET}
(skip), @kbd{a} (execute this step and all remaining steps without
further prompts), or @kbd{q} (abort).

@item --model / -m
Override @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} for this invocation only.  The model must
already be downloaded --- Codebud never runs @command{ollama pull} on
your behalf.  It validates and warms the model (blocking, with the
usual progress spinner) before planning starts, so a bad name fails
fast instead of wasting a planning cycle:

@example
codebud run -m qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M "your request"
@end example

@strong{Note:} @option{--model} must come @emph{after} the subcommand
name, not before (@pxref{Global Options}).

@item --no-rag
Plan without project context from the index, as if @env{CODEBUD_RAG} were
@samp{0} (@pxref{The index Subcommand}).
@end table


@node The plan Subcommand
@section The @command{plan} Subcommand
@cindex plan subcommand
@cindex inspecting a plan

@command{plan} generates a plan and prints it without executing anything.
Use it to check what Codebud @emph{intends} to do before letting it act.

@example
codebud plan "@var{your request}"
@end example

Example:

@example
codebud plan "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

To see the raw JSON instead of the formatted display:

@example
codebud plan --json "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

@noindent
The JSON format is exactly what the executor receives.  Each step has
the fields @code{id}, @code{description}, @code{tool}, and @code{args}.

To also execute the plan after showing it --- printing full,
untruncated step output the same way @command{run} does --- pass
@option{--execute} (or @option{-x}):

@example
codebud plan -x "delete all .pyc files"
@end example

@noindent
The steps follow the same rules as in @command{run}: powerful commands
and web searches ask @samp{Run it? [y/N]} first, and without a terminal
they are skipped and @command{plan} exits with status 1, unless you add
@option{--yes} (or @option{-y}).

@option{--execute} and @option{--json} cannot be combined: executing
steps has side effects, and @option{--json} output is meant to be
piped into scripts, so mixing the two would be ambiguous.  @command{plan}
also accepts @option{--model}/@option{-m}, with the same validate-then-warm
behaviour as @command{run}, and @option{--no-rag} (@pxref{The run
Subcommand}).


@node The chat Subcommand
@section The @command{chat} Subcommand
@cindex chat subcommand
@cindex interactive session
@cindex multi-turn

@command{chat} opens an interactive session.  Each request you type
is planned and executed, and the memory store accumulates the history
across turns so Codebud can refer to what it did earlier in the same
session.

@example
codebud chat
@end example

After each plan is shown, @command{chat} asks
@samp{Run this plan? [y/N/r(evise)[: note]]}: @kbd{y} runs it, @kbd{n} or
@key{RET} skips it, and @kbd{r} (optionally @kbd{r: @var{note}}) asks the
model for a revised plan.  In the default @code{ask} mode it then walks you
through every step one at a time.  For each step you can press:

@table @kbd
@item y @key{RET}
Execute this step.
@item @key{RET} (or @kbd{n})
Skip this step without executing.
@item a @key{RET}
Execute this step and all remaining steps automatically.
@item q @key{RET}
Abort the current plan and return to the prompt.
@end table

Other modes change these prompts: @code{auto} runs the steps after the
plan is approved and asks only before powerful commands and web searches,
@code{full-auto} asks nothing, and @code{plan} never runs anything
(@pxref{Agent Modes}).

To end the session, type @samp{quit}, @samp{exit} or @samp{q}, or press
@kbd{Ctrl-D}.  An empty line (@key{RET} alone) just shows the prompt again.

On a terminal, a dim horizontal rule is drawn across the full width
before each @samp{[@var{n}] You:} prompt, so turns are easy to tell
apart:

@example
────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[1] You: list the python files
@end example

The rule is omitted when standard error is not a terminal (for example
when it is redirected to a file), and drawn with @samp{-} if the
terminal's encoding cannot show @samp{─}.

@strong{Why use chat instead of run?}  When you are working through a
multi-step task interactively --- for example, exploring a codebase,
then editing a file, then running the tests --- @command{chat} lets
each turn build on the previous one.  Memory from earlier turns helps
the planner make better decisions.

@subheading The input line
@cindex toolbar
@cindex slash command menu

On a terminal, the @samp{[@var{n}] You:} line has a persistent toolbar at
the bottom of the screen showing the agent mode, the privacy level, the
model, how full
the context window is and, when the project has an index, its state
(@samp{RAG}, @samp{RAG stale} when files changed since it was built, or
@samp{RAG off}; @pxref{The index Subcommand}):

@example
[3] You: _

 mode: ask · privacy: private · qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M · context 14% · RAG   (shift-tab: mode)
@end example

@itemize @bullet
@item
Typing @samp{/} opens a menu of slash commands with a short description of
each; it narrows as you type, and @key{Up}/@key{Down} and @key{Tab} pick an
entry.
@item
@kbd{Shift-Tab} cycles the mode (@code{ask}, @code{auto},
@code{full-auto}, @code{plan}, @code{edit}); the toolbar updates immediately (@pxref{Agent Modes}).
@item
@key{Up} and @key{Down} on an empty line recall earlier input.
@end itemize

The toolbar is shown while Codebud waits for your input, not while a plan
runs.  When standard input or output is not a terminal (for example when
input is piped in), Codebud reads plain lines without the menu or
toolbar.

@subheading Slash commands

Any input starting with @samp{/} is handled locally by the REPL and
never sent to the model:

@multitable @columnfractions .25 .75
@headitem Command @tab Effect
@item @code{/help} @tab Print the command reference.
@item @code{/history} @tab Show the conversation turns kept in memory.
@item @code{/clear} @tab Wipe conversation history and reset the turn counter.
@item @code{/undo} @tab Remove the last user+assistant turn pair.
@item @code{/mode} @tab Show the agent mode, or @code{/mode @var{name}} to
switch (@pxref{Agent Modes}).
@item @code{/save} @tab Save the session now; @code{/save @var{name}} also
gives it a name to resume by (@pxref{Saved Sessions}).
@item @code{/compact} @tab Replace older turns with a short model-written
summary, keeping the last exchange verbatim (@pxref{Compacting a Conversation}).
@item @code{/privacy} @tab Show the privacy level, or
@code{/privacy @var{level}} (@code{locked}, @code{private},
@code{trusted}) to switch (@pxref{Privacy Levels}).
@item @code{/profile} @tab Show the profile, or @code{/profile @var{name}}
to switch: its mode, privacy level, policy and prompt apply at once
(@pxref{Profiles}).
@item @code{/rag} @tab Show whether requests get project context and how
fresh the index is; @code{/rag on} or @code{/rag off} switches it for this
session (@pxref{The index Subcommand}).
@item @code{/sources} @tab List the project files (with line ranges) the
last request was given as context.
@item @code{/index} @tab Build or refresh the project index;
@code{/index status} shows what changed since, @code{/index rebuild}
starts over.
@item @code{/doctor} @tab Run environment health checks inline.
@item @code{/model} @tab Show the active model.
@item @code{/model @var{name}} @tab Switch models mid-session.
@item @code{/models} @tab List downloaded models, marking the active one.
@item @code{/plan} @tab Re-display the last received plan.
@item @code{/output} @tab Show the full stdout of the last executed step.
@end multitable

@anchor{Agent Modes}
@subheading Agent modes
@cindex modes

A @dfn{mode} controls what Codebud may do.  Start in one with
@option{--mode}, switch with @code{/mode @var{name}}, and check the current
one with @code{/mode} (it is also shown in the session header):

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .88
@headitem Mode @tab Behaviour
@item @code{ask} @tab Default.  Review the plan, then approve each step.
@item @code{auto} @tab Review the plan once; its steps then run without
per-step prompts, except powerful commands (code runners such as
@code{pytest}, file-changing commands such as @code{cp}) and web searches
(which send their query off the machine), which still ask
@samp{Run it? [y/N]}.
@item @code{full-auto} @tab No prompts at all: the plan runs as soon as it
is made, powerful commands and web searches included (each query is still
printed).  For when you do not want to be
interrupted.  The allow-list and refused arguments still apply, so
commands outside the lists are still rejected.
@item @code{plan} @tab Plans are shown but never executed.
@item @code{edit} @tab Only file tools (@code{file_read}, @code{file_write},
@code{patch}); shell commands are refused.
@end multitable

@noindent
The mode says how much Codebud asks; what it may reach is a separate dial,
the privacy level (@pxref{Privacy Levels}).

@example
codebud chat --mode plan
[1] You: /mode edit
  Mode: edit
[1] You: run the test suite

Plan error: The plan uses tools not allowed in edit mode: command.
  Run `codebud doctor` to check your environment.
@end example

Restrictions are enforced by the safety engine and again just before each
step runs, so switching to a stricter mode also stops a plan made earlier
(@pxref{SafetyEngine}).  The mode is saved with the session and restored
by @option{--resume}; an explicit @option{--mode} takes precedence.

@anchor{Saved Sessions}
@subheading Saved sessions

Every chat is saved automatically to @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/sessions/}
(by default @file{~/.local/share/codebud/sessions/};
@pxref{Files and Directories}) after each turn
and when it ends, readable only by you and with secrets such as API keys
masked as @samp{[redacted]}; the last line printed tells you how to
resume it:

@example
[4] You: quit
  Session saved: 20261025-093012-7f3a  (resume with: codebud chat --resume 20261025-093012-7f3a)
@end example

@code{/save @var{name}} gives the session a memorable name (letters,
digits, @samp{.}, @samp{_} and @samp{-}; unique across sessions), which
can be used instead of the ID.  A chat that ends without any turns is not
saved.  Several chats can run at once; each has its own session file.
To continue a saved conversation, use @option{--resume}:

@example
codebud chat --resume                      @r{# the most recent session}
codebud chat --resume refactor-memory      @r{# by name}
codebud chat --resume 20261025-093012-7f3a @r{# by ID}
@end example

The history is restored, the last exchange is shown so you can see where
you left off, and the conversation keeps saving to the same session.
The session's model is restored too, if it is still downloaded;
otherwise a warning is shown and the current model is used.  An explicit
@option{--model} takes precedence.  Without an argument, @option{--resume}
picks the most recently updated session that is not open elsewhere.

A session can only be open in one chat at a time; resuming one that
another chat has open fails with @samp{Cannot resume: Session
'@dots{}' is open in another codebud chat.}  @command{codebud sessions}
lists what is available (@pxref{The sessions Subcommand}).

@xref{Sessions}, for the file format and how concurrent chats are kept
apart.

@anchor{Compacting a Conversation}
@subheading Compacting a conversation

@code{/compact} asks the model for a short summary of everything except the
last exchange (your latest request and Codebud's answer), then replaces those
older turns with it.  @code{/history} shows the result as a @samp{SUMMARY}
entry:

@example
[3] You: /compact
  Compacted 2 entries (72 chars) into a 80-char summary; kept the last 2.
[3] You: /history
  [1] SUMMARY: The user asked the coding agent to list all Python files ...
  [2] USER: show the last 3 git commits
  [3] ASSISTANT: planned: command(git log -n 3)
@end example

Use it when a long session has made the prompt large: a smaller prompt is
faster to process and leaves more room in the context window
(@env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX}).  Compaction cannot be undone; if the model returns
no summary, history is left unchanged.  Codebud also drops the oldest turns
automatically when a prompt would not fit, so @code{/compact} is about keeping
the gist of older turns rather than preventing errors.

@cindex context warning
After each turn, @command{chat} estimates the size of the next planning
prompt, including the project context the last request retrieved.  Once
it reaches 70% of the context window, a warning is shown (once per turn,
not after slash commands):

@example
  Context 72% full (~5,900 of 8,192 tokens). Use /compact to summarise older turns.
@end example

With the default @env{CODEBUD_NUM_CTX} of 8,192 tokens and a first-turn
prompt of about 1,080 tokens, this only appears after a substantial
conversation.

@code{/model @var{name}} validates the model is downloaded and blocks
on a live warmup (with the usual spinner) before switching --- if
either check fails, the active model is left unchanged and the reason
is printed:

@example
[3] You: /model qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M
| Thinking... 4s (prefill)
  Switched to 'qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M'.
@end example

@code{/models} is the @command{chat}-mode equivalent of the
@command{models} subcommand (@pxref{The models Subcommand}), useful
when you want to see what is downloaded without leaving the session.


@node The doctor Subcommand
@section The @command{doctor} Subcommand
@cindex doctor subcommand
@cindex environment check

@command{doctor} checks your environment and tells you what is wrong
before you try to run an agent call.  Run it any time things are not
working.

@example
codebud doctor
@end example

It checks:

@enumerate
@item Python version (3.10 or later required)
@item The @code{codebud} package is importable
@item Which LLM backend gets the prompts, and whether it is local
(@env{CODEBUD_BACKEND}; fails for an unknown backend, @code{openai}
without @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}, or a remote Ollama over plain http that is
not allowed)
@item The privacy level, and whether the backend fits it (fails at
@code{locked} with a backend that is not on this machine)
@item How command steps are isolated (bubblewrap, @command{unshare}, or a
warning that they are not --- a failure at @code{locked})
@item Where data can leave this machine: the OpenAI API, a remote Ollama
host, DuckDuckGo for web search --- or @samp{Outbound: none}
@item @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} environment variable is set
@item The Ollama server is reachable at @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL}
(default @code{http://localhost:11434})
@item The selected model has been pulled
@item The embedding model (@env{EMBED_MODEL}, default
      @code{all-minilm}) has been pulled --- a warning only, since
      it is needed just for project search
@item The data and configuration directories are private (owner only) ---
      a warning with the @command{chmod} that fixes it otherwise
@item Encryption at rest: whether it is on, and the key --- a failure
      when it is on without a key or without @code{pyrage}, and a warning
      while @env{CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE} is set (@pxref{Encryption at Rest})
@item The policy files in effect --- @file{harmless_commands.json},
      @file{powerful_commands.json} and @file{trusted_sequences.json},
      from @file{~/.config/codebud/} or the shipped defaults, and the
      project's @file{.codebud/} ones --- exist and are valid JSON, and
      whether a project policy is trusted
@item Enough free disk space near @file{~/.ollama/models/}
@item Enough free RAM for the selected model (Linux only; skipped where
      @file{/proc/meminfo} does not exist)
@end enumerate

Each failed check prints a one-line fix.  Warnings print
@samp{[WARN]} with a fix too, but do not make @command{doctor} fail.
Example output when
@env{OLLAMA_MODEL} is not set:

@example
  [FAIL] OLLAMA_MODEL is not set
         -> Run: export OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
@end example

@strong{Run @command{doctor} first} whenever you see a
@code{plan_error} or a connection failure.  It saves time.


@node The history Subcommand
@section The @command{history} Subcommand
@cindex history subcommand
@cindex execution history

@command{history} lists steps that Codebud has executed, across all
sessions, newest last.  Every completed step of @command{run},
@command{plan --execute} and @command{chat} is recorded.

@example
codebud history             @r{# the 20 most recent entries}
codebud history -n 5        @r{# the 5 most recent}
codebud history -n 0        @r{# everything}
codebud history --json      @r{# raw entries, for scripts}
@end example

Example output:

@example
[1] 2026-10-25T09:12:03.418245+00:00  command          ok       list files
@end example

Each line shows the time (UTC), the tool used, the outcome (@samp{ok} or
@samp{exit @var{n}}) and the step description.

This is not the same as @code{/history} inside @command{chat}, which
shows the conversation turns of the current session only
(@pxref{The chat Subcommand}).

The entries are stored as JSON Lines in
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/memory/entries.jsonl}, which @command{history} reads without
contacting Ollama.  @xref{MemoryStore}, for the file format and how it is
kept safe against crashes and concurrent writers.

@command{codebud history --purge} deletes the history after listing the
files and asking (@option{--yes} to skip the question, e.g.@: in scripts).
That includes older copies of the same history kept in plain text: a
pre-2026 @file{entries.json} or its @file{entries.json.migrated} copy,
and any @file{*.corrupt-*} file set aside after damage.


@node The sessions Subcommand
@section The @command{sessions} Subcommand
@cindex sessions subcommand

@command{sessions} lists saved @command{chat} sessions, most recently
updated first, with what you need to resume one:

@example
codebud sessions            @r{# the 20 most recent}
codebud sessions -n 0       @r{# all of them}
codebud sessions --json     @r{# raw summaries, for scripts}
@end example

Example output:

@example
20261025-040741-9dcb  live-check  (open)
  updated 2026-10-25T04:07:41  ·  4 entries  ·  qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
  list the python files in this project

Resume with: codebud chat --resume <id or name>
@end example

@samp{(open)} marks a session that another chat currently has open; it
cannot be resumed until that chat ends.  @xref{Saved Sessions}.

@cindex deleting sessions
@cindex session retention
Sessions are kept until you delete them:

@example
codebud sessions --delete work           @r{# one session, by ID or name}
codebud sessions --purge                 @r{# every session not open in a chat}
codebud sessions --purge --older-than 30 @r{# only those unused for 30+ days}
@end example

@noindent
Each lists the files it would remove and asks @samp{[y/N]}; without a
terminal it deletes nothing unless you add @option{--yes}.  A session
open in another chat is never deleted.  @option{--purge} also removes
session files Codebud cannot read (damaged, or not decryptable with your
key); with @option{--older-than} their age is unknown, so they are kept
and listed instead.  To have old sessions removed
automatically, set @code{session_retention_days} in your
@file{config.toml} (or @env{CODEBUD_SESSION_RETENTION_DAYS}): each
@command{chat} then starts by deleting sessions not used for that many
days, and says which.  The default, @code{0}, keeps them forever; a
project's @file{config.toml} cannot set it.


@node The index Subcommand
@section The @command{index} Subcommand
@cindex index subcommand
@cindex project index
@cindex embeddings

@command{index} reads your project, splits it into chunks and stores
an embedding vector for each, so Codebud can later find the code and
documentation relevant to a request.

@cindex project context
Once a project has an index, every request from @command{run},
@command{plan} and @command{chat} first searches it, and the five most
relevant chunks go into the planning prompt as @dfn{project context},
marked as reference data rather than instructions.  The block is fenced
by a random tag chosen for each request, so text in a file cannot fake
its end and pose as your request, and lines that imitate the prompt's
own structure (@samp{USER REQUEST:} and the like) are labelled
@samp{(file text)}.  This is what lets the
planner name real files: asked to change a function, it reads and patches
the file that defines it instead of guessing a path.  The context takes at
most a quarter of the context window, and is the first thing dropped when
the prompt is full (conversation history is kept).  Plain chat replies,
given when no plan can be made (typically questions about the code), get
the same context and are asked to cite the files they use as
@samp{path:start-end}: asked which files are never indexed as secrets, the
3B model listed them exactly and named @file{src/codebud/rag/files.py},
where without the context it made up an answer.

The context costs time: on a CPU, its roughly 2,000 extra prompt tokens
can add half a minute to a cold request.  Set @env{CODEBUD_RAG} to
@samp{0} to turn it off; without an index, or without @code{numpy},
requests simply go ahead without it.

In @command{chat}, @code{/sources} lists the chunks the last request was
given, @code{/rag off} and @code{/rag on} switch the context for the rest
of the session, and @code{/index} builds or refreshes the index without
leaving the chat; @command{run}, @command{plan} and @command{chat} also
take @option{--no-rag}.

@cindex index, stale
Codebud never re-indexes by itself, since that can take minutes.  Instead
the chat toolbar shows @samp{RAG stale} when files changed since the last
@code{/index}, and a one-line hint suggests @code{/index}: when a chat
starts in a git project without an index, when it starts with an
out-of-date index, and after a plan's steps changed files (once, until you
next run @code{/index}).  These checks look only at file names and
modification times, so a file that was touched but not changed also
counts; refreshing it then embeds nothing.  Set @env{CODEBUD_RAG_HINTS}
to @samp{0} to turn the hints off.

Small models make the least of it: the 3B model answers questions about
the code well with it, but plans edits badly even when the right file is
in its context; the 7B model planned five of five test edits against the
right file (@pxref{Planning with Project Context}).

@example
codebud index               @r{# build or refresh the index}
codebud index ~/src/proj    @r{# a different project}
codebud index --status      @r{# what is indexed, and what changed since}
codebud index --rebuild     @r{# start over (e.g.@: after changing EMBED_MODEL)}
codebud index --drop        @r{# delete the index (asks first; --yes to skip)}
@end example

Without a path, the project is the git work tree containing the current
directory (or the current directory outside git).  It needs the embedding
model, @code{all-minilm} by default (@pxref{Pulling a Model}).

The first run is the slow one: on a CPU, Codebud's own repository (about
95 files, 1,180 chunks) takes about a minute with @code{all-minilm}, and
about nine minutes with @code{nomic-embed-text}.  A progress line shows
which file is being read.  Later runs only embed what changed.

@subsection What gets indexed

Inside git, the files git sees as part of the project: tracked files plus
new files that are not ignored, so @file{.gitignore} and
@file{.git/info/exclude} apply.  Outside git, every file under the
directory, except hidden directories, virtual environments, caches and
build output.  Then these are always skipped:

@itemize
@item Anything that looks like a secret, whatever the ignore files say:
@file{.env} and @file{.envrc} files, keys and key stores (@file{*.pem},
@file{*.key}, @file{id_rsa*}, @file{*.p12}, @dots{}), credential files
(@file{.netrc}, @file{.pypirc}, @file{.git-credentials},
@file{.vault-token}, @file{.htpasswd}, @file{.s3cfg}, @file{.boto},
@file{.dockercfg}, names containing @samp{credential} or @samp{secret}),
shell and REPL histories (@file{.bash_history}, @dots{}), Terraform state
and variables (@file{*.tfstate}, @file{*.tfvars}), encrypted files, and
anything under @file{.ssh}, @file{.gnupg}, @file{.aws}, @file{.azure},
@file{.kube}, @file{.docker}, @file{.password-store} or @file{.terraform}.
@item Patterns listed in a @file{.codebudignore} file at the project
root: one per line, shell-style (@samp{*.generated.py}), a trailing
@samp{/} for a directory (@samp{vendor/}), @samp{#} for comments.
@item Symbolic links, lock files, minified files, source maps, files
larger than 256@tie{}KB, and anything that is not UTF-8 text.
@end itemize

@noindent
A secret inside an ordinary file --- an API key in @file{config.py}, an
@code{aws_secret_access_key} in @file{aws.cfg} --- is masked as
@samp{[redacted]} before the text is embedded and stored, the same way
sessions and history are masked (@pxref{Files and Directories}), so it
never reaches a prompt.  With masking turned off
(@env{CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS=0}) the index keeps the text as it is.  An
index built before this masking existed keeps its old text until
@command{codebud index --rebuild}.

Python files are split into their top-level functions and classes (a
long class into its methods), Texinfo files by node, Markdown by heading,
and other text into overlapping 40-line windows.

@subsection Refreshing

Running @command{codebud index} again only re-embeds files whose
contents changed, and forgets deleted ones, so a refresh is quick.  Each
file is saved as soon as it is done: if indexing is interrupted (or the
embedding model goes away), what finished is kept and the next run
continues.  @option{--status} shows how many files changed since the last
run:

@example
Index of /home/you/src/codebud
  model          all-minilm (384-dim)
  contents       87 files, 1061 chunks
  keyword search yes
  updated        2026-10-27T15:04:11+00:00
  file           /home/you/.local/share/codebud/index/3f2a9c0d1e7b5a64/index.sqlite3
  encrypted      no
  since then     2 changed, 1 new, 0 deleted -- refresh with: codebud index
@end example

An index belongs to one embedding model.  If you change
@env{EMBED_MODEL}, @command{codebud index} refuses to mix the two and asks
for @option{--rebuild}.

@subsection Where the index lives

One SQLite database per project, under
@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud/index/} (by default
@file{~/.local/share/codebud/index/}), in a directory named after a hash of
the project's path.  The index holds copies of your files' text, so the
directory is readable only by you (mode 0700, database 0600).  It never
leaves your machine; the embeddings are computed by your local Ollama.

With encryption at rest on, a new index is stored encrypted as
@file{index.sqlite3.age} and used from a copy in memory under
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}; refreshing it or showing @option{--status} asks
for the passphrase once, building a new one or @option{--rebuild} does
not (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).


@node The skill Subcommand
@section The @command{skill} Subcommand
@cindex skill subcommand
@cindex Claude Code
@cindex OpenClaw

Codebud ships @dfn{skills}: @file{SKILL.md} files that teach another agent
when to delegate a coding task to Codebud and how to call it (including
@code{--no-progress} and @code{--yes} for unattended use).  The default
skill is for Claude Code and OpenClaw (install into their personal skills
directories).  An Agensi marketplace variant adds install guidance for
users who do not have Codebud yet; print it with @option{--for agensi} and
upload a ZIP that has @file{SKILL.md} at the root.

@example
codebud skill show                       @r{# default skill (Claude / OpenClaw)}
codebud skill show --for agensi          @r{# Agensi hand-off skill}
codebud skill install --for claude       @r{# ~/.claude/skills/codebud/}
codebud skill install --for openclaw     @r{# ~/.openclaw/skills/codebud/}
codebud skill install --dir @var{path}       @r{# any other directory}
@end example

Installing again is harmless when the installed copy is identical.  If it
differs --- an older version, or one you edited --- Codebud refuses and
leaves it alone; add @option{--force} to replace it.  A @file{SKILL.md}
that is a symbolic link is never written through, even with
@option{--force}, and the file is replaced all at once, never left half
written.


@node The models Subcommand
@section The @command{models} Subcommand
@cindex models subcommand

@command{models} lists all Ollama models that are currently downloaded
and marks which one is selected by @env{OLLAMA_MODEL}.

@example
codebud models
@end example

Example output:

@example
NAME                                       SIZE  SELECTED
  qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M        1.8G  <-- active
  qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M        4.7G
@end example

To switch models, pass @option{--model}/@option{-m} on @command{run},
@command{plan}, or @command{chat}, or type @code{/model @var{name}}
inside an active @command{chat} session (@pxref{The chat Subcommand}).
Both paths validate the model is downloaded and warm it before
switching --- Codebud never runs @command{ollama pull} on your behalf,
and never touches @env{OLLAMA_MODEL} directly on a failed switch.


@node The config Subcommand
@section The @command{config} Subcommand
@cindex config subcommand
@cindex resolved configuration

@command{config} prints the value of every setting Codebud reads and where
it came from: the environment, a configuration file
(@pxref{Configuration File}) or the default.

@example
codebud config
@end example

Example output:

@example
Codebud resolved configuration:
  OLLAMA_MODEL              = qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M   (environment)
  OLLAMA_BASE_URL           = http://localhost:11434             (default)
  EMBED_MODEL               = all-minilm                         (default)
  CODEBUD_RAG               = 1                                  (default)
  CODEBUD_RAG_HINTS         = 1                                  (default)
  CODEBUD_NUM_CTX           = 16384                              (user config)
  OPENAI_API_KEY            = (not set)                          (default)
  OPENAI_MODEL              = gpt-3.5-turbo                      (default)
  WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED        = 0                                  (default)

Files:
  config file              = /home/you/.config/codebud/config.toml
  data directory           = /home/you/.local/share/codebud
  harmless_commands.json   = /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/codebud/data/policy/harmless_commands.json
  powerful_commands.json   = /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/codebud/data/policy/powerful_commands.json
  trusted_sequences.json   = /usr/local/lib/python3.14/site-packages/codebud/data/policy/trusted_sequences.json
@end example

Use this to confirm that your settings are actually being picked up, and
to see which configuration and policy files are in effect
(@pxref{Policy JSON Files}).  A privacy level set by a profile shows the
profile as its source.


@node The profiles Subcommand
@section The @command{profiles} Subcommand
@cindex profiles subcommand

@command{profiles} lists the built-in profiles and your own, with their
mode, privacy level, description and file; @samp{*} marks the active one
(@pxref{Profiles}).

@example
$ codebud profiles
* default        ask, privacy private         Ask before each step; data stays where you chose.
                 built in
  unattended     full-auto, privacy trusted   Fully unattended: nothing is asked, steps are not isolated. The allow-list still applies.
                 built in

Your own profiles: /home/you/.config/codebud/profiles/<name>.toml
@end example


@node The trust Subcommand
@section The @command{trust} Subcommand
@cindex trust subcommand

@command{trust} shows how the current project's @file{.codebud/} policy
would widen yours, flagging risky entries, and asks whether to trust it
(@pxref{Project Policy}).  @option{--yes} trusts without asking;
@option{--revoke} stops trusting it.  A project whose policy only narrows
yours needs no trust.

@example
$ codebud trust
/home/you/src/app/.codebud: not trusted (only its narrowing applies). It widens your policy with:
    harmless: make test (new)
    powerful: cargo (new)
Trust this project's policy? [y/N] y
Trusted. Codebud asks again if its policy files change.
@end example


@node The key Subcommand
@section The @command{key} Subcommand
@cindex key subcommand

@command{key} manages the age key that encrypts data at rest
(@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).  It needs the optional @code{pyrage} module.

@table @command
@item codebud key init
Create the key in @file{~/.config/codebud/keys/}, sealed with a new
passphrase of at least 12 characters, typed twice (or taken from
@env{CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE}).  Refuses if a key already exists.
@item codebud key passwd
Change the passphrase: asks for the current one, then the new one twice.
The key, and so everything encrypted with it, stays the same.
@item codebud key status
Whether encryption is on and available, where the key is, its public
half, and where the passphrase will come from.
@item codebud key encrypt-existing [--yes]
Encrypt sessions, step history and project indexes saved in plain text
before encryption
was turned on.  Each original is first copied, unchanged, into a
@file{plaintext-backup-@var{time}/} directory that Codebud lists and
never deletes; then the live file is replaced by its encrypted version.
Asks first; sessions open in a chat are skipped.
@end table

@example
$ codebud key init
New passphrase:
Repeat it:
Created the encryption key in /home/you/.config/codebud/keys
  public key age1@dots{}
Keep the passphrase safe: without it, data encrypted to this key cannot be recovered by anyone, codebud included.
@end example


@node The version Subcommand
@section The @command{version} Subcommand
@cindex version subcommand

@command{version} shows the Codebud version, the running Ollama server
version, the active model, and the Python version.

@example
codebud version
@end example

Example output:

@example
codebud  0.1.0
ollama   0.40.6
model    qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M
python   3.14.8
@end example

The Codebud version is read from the installed package's metadata, so it
always matches @file{pyproject.toml}.  Include this output when reporting
bugs.


@node Progress Output
@section Progress Output
@cindex progress indicator
@cindex spinner
@cindex prefill phase
@cindex token rate

When Codebud sends a request to the model, it displays a live progress
indicator in the terminal.  There are two phases:

@table @strong
@item Prefill phase (the slow part)
The model is processing your input prompt.  No output has arrived yet.
The display shows a spinning character and an elapsed-time counter:

@example
| Thinking... 47s (prefill)
@end example

On a CPU-only laptop, prefill for the 3B model takes about 30 seconds for
the first planning request of a session and about 2 seconds for later ones
(Ollama reuses the cached start of the prompt).  @emph{This is normal.}
The model is not frozen.

@item Generation phase
The model starts producing tokens.  The display switches to a token
counter and speed estimate:

@example
/ Generating... tokens: 42 | 10.3 t/s | 34s elapsed
@end example
@end table

When the model finishes, a summary line replaces the spinner:

@example
prefill: 30s | gen: 5s | total: 35s | tokens: 48 | 10.3 t/s
@end example

@noindent
The progress indicator is written to standard error, so it does not
interfere with piped output.  To suppress it entirely, pass
@option{--no-progress} (before the subcommand); it is also disabled
automatically when standard error is not a terminal.


@node Reading a Plan
@section Reading a Plan
@cindex plan display
@cindex reading a plan

After the model finishes, Codebud prints a numbered plan summary.
Example:

@example
Plan (3 steps)
  [1] file_read          read README.md
       /home/you/proj/README.md
  [2] command            run pytest -q
       pytest -q
  [3] file_write         write test-summary.md
       /home/you/proj/test-summary.md (812 bytes)
@end example

Each step shows its number, the tool and the model's description of it,
and under that what it really acts on, taken from its arguments: the
resolved file path (with @samp{->} when a symlink leads elsewhere), the
command line (@samp{[network]} when it asked for the network), the
search query.  Read that second line: the description is only the
model's account of the step, and text in your project can try to make
it misleading.  When a @code{patch} step runs, its whole diff is shown
first.  Control characters in anything shown are printed as escapes
(@samp{\x1b}), never obeyed.  The tool names correspond to the built-in
tools described in @ref{Tools Reference}.

If the model cannot produce a valid plan, Codebud answers with a plain
conversational reply instead (@pxref{No Plan or an Empty Reply}).  Errors
that do reach you are printed with an explanation and a fix when Codebud
recognises them, for example a plan refused because of the agent mode:

@example
Plan error: The plan uses tools not allowed in edit mode: command.
  Run `codebud doctor` to check your environment.
@end example

The hints mirror the @ref{Troubleshooting} chapter.


@node Reading Step Results
@section Reading Step Results
@cindex step results
@cindex execution output

After each step executes, Codebud prints a one-line outcome, followed
by the step's standard output:

@example
  -> ok, 12 lines stdout
line 1
line 2
@dots{}
@end example

Or, if the step failed:

@example
  -> exit 1, stderr: pytest: no tests ran
@end example

@strong{@command{run} and @command{plan -x}} always print the full,
untruncated stdout of every step --- there is no follow-up prompt in a
one-shot invocation to ask for more, so nothing is ever hidden by
default.

@strong{@command{chat}} shows only the first 15 lines of stdout by
default, with a note when more was withheld:

@example
  -> ok, 42 lines stdout
line 1
@dots{}
line 15
  ... (+27 more lines, see /output or --verbose)
@end example

Type @code{/output} to see the rest of the @emph{last} executed step's
output, or start the chat with @command{codebud -v chat} to always show
full output for every step in the session.

@cindex control characters
A step's output, the model's replies and error messages are not
Codebud's own text, so control characters in them are shown as escapes
(@samp{\x1b}, @samp{\r}, @samp{\u202e}) rather than obeyed: an escape
sequence or carriage return cannot move the cursor and rewrite what you
are reading or approving, and a right-to-left override cannot make text
read differently than it is.  Newlines, tabs and emoji are kept.  When
the output of @command{run} or @command{plan} is piped rather than shown
on a terminal, a chat reply on standard output is passed through
unchanged, for scripts.


@node Verbose and JSON Output
@section Verbose and JSON Output
@cindex verbose output
@cindex JSON output

@table @option
@item --verbose / -v
Show each step's arguments in the plan display (@command{run},
@command{plan}, @command{chat}), and in @command{chat} the full stdout of
each step instead of the 15-line preview; @command{run} and
@command{plan -x} always show full output (@pxref{Reading Step Results}).
Must come before the subcommand: @command{codebud -v chat}.

@item --no-progress
Suppress the spinner entirely.  Useful when running Codebud from a
script or when redirecting output.  Must come before the subcommand.

@item codebud plan --json "@var{request}"
Print the raw JSON plan instead of the formatted display.  Useful for
piping into other tools or for inspecting the exact @code{args} the
planner chose.  Cannot be combined with @option{--execute}.
@end table


@node Global Options
@section Global Options
@cindex global options

@option{--no-progress}, @option{--privacy}, @option{--profile} and
@option{--verbose} belong to @command{codebud} itself, so they must come
@emph{before} the subcommand name (@command{codebud chat -v} is
rejected):

@example
codebud [--no-progress] [--privacy @var{level}] [--profile @var{name}] [--verbose] @var{subcommand} ...
@end example

@table @option
@item --no-progress
Disable the live spinner and timing summary.  The agent still runs;
only the progress display is suppressed.

@item --privacy locked|private|trusted
What Codebud may reach for this run, overriding @file{config.toml}:
@code{locked} keeps everything on this machine, @code{trusted} drops the
privacy prompts (@pxref{Privacy Levels}).

@item --profile @var{name}
Start with this profile: its mode, privacy level, policy files and
planner instructions.  Built in: @code{default} and @code{unattended}
(@pxref{Profiles}).

@item --verbose / -v
Show step arguments in plans and, in @command{chat}, full step output
for every step in the session.

@item --help / -h
Print a usage summary and exit.
@end table

@strong{@option{--model}/@option{-m} is different:} it is defined
@emph{per subcommand}, not globally, and must come @emph{after} the
subcommand name:

@example
codebud run --model qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M "your request"   @r{@i{(correct)}}
codebud --model qwen2.5-coder:7b-instruct-q4_K_M run "your request"   @r{@i{(error: invalid choice)}}
@end example

This is deliberate, not an oversight: @command{argparse} lets a
subcommand's own default silently overwrite a same-named option
inherited from before the subcommand, so @option{--model} is only
declared where it is actually read (@command{run}, @command{plan},
@command{chat}) to avoid that trap entirely.


@node Environment Variables
@section Environment Variables
@cindex environment variables

Codebud is configured through the environment variables below, plus the
safety policy files (@pxref{Policy JSON Files}).

@table @env
@item OLLAMA_MODEL
@strong{Required.}  The model name exactly as it appears in
@command{ollama list} (and @command{codebud models}).  Example:
@code{qwen2.5-coder:3b-instruct-q4_K_M}.  Can be overridden per
invocation with @option{--model}/@option{-m} on @command{run},
@command{plan}, or @command{chat}, or switched mid-session with
@code{/model @var{name}} in @command{chat} --- both validate the model
is downloaded and warm it before changing this variable
(@pxref{The chat Subcommand}).

@item OLLAMA_BASE_URL
The base URL of the Ollama REST API.  Default:
@code{http://localhost:11434}.  Change this only if Ollama is running
on a different machine or port; another machine must be reached over
https or an SSH tunnel, or allowed with @env{CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP}
(@pxref{LLM Backend Variables}).

@item CODEBUD_ALLOW_REMOTE_HTTP
Set to @code{1} to allow a remote @env{OLLAMA_BASE_URL} over plain http.
Default: @code{0}.

@item CODEBUD_PRIVACY
@code{locked}, @code{private} (default) or @code{trusted}: what Codebud may
reach (@pxref{Privacy Levels}).

@item CODEBUD_PROFILE
The profile to start with, by name (@pxref{Profiles}).  Default:
@code{default}.

@item CODEBUD_SESSION_RETENTION_DAYS
Default: @code{0} (keep sessions forever).  A positive number makes each
@command{chat} start by deleting sessions not used for that many days
(@pxref{The sessions Subcommand}).

@item CODEBUD_MASK_SECRETS
Default: @code{1}: API keys, tokens, private keys and passwords are saved
as @samp{[redacted]} in sessions and step history.  Set to @code{0} to
store them verbatim (@pxref{Files and Directories}).

@item CODEBUD_ENCRYPT
Default: @code{0}.  @code{1} encrypts chat sessions, the step history and
new project indexes
with your age key (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).

@item CODEBUD_STEP_ENV
Empty by default.  More environment variables for command steps, which
otherwise get only an allow-list (@pxref{Isolated Steps}).

@item CODEBUD_PASSPHRASE
Not set by default.  The passphrase of the encryption key, for unattended
runs only; otherwise Codebud asks on the terminal.  @command{codebud
doctor} warns while it is set (@pxref{Encryption at Rest}).  It is never
read from @file{config.toml}.

@item CODEBUD_NUM_CTX
The context window, in tokens, requested from Ollama on every call.
Default: @code{8192}.  Raise it if you work with long conversations or
large files; see @ref{Environment Variables Overview} for the memory
cost.

@item EMBED_MODEL
The dedicated embedding model used by the @code{search_embeddings}
tool.  Defaults to @code{all-minilm},
independent of @env{OLLAMA_MODEL}.

@item CODEBUD_BACKEND
@code{ollama} (default) or @code{openai}: which backend gets the prompts.
There is no fallback between them (@pxref{LLM Backend Variables}).

@item OPENAI_API_KEY
The API key for the @code{openai} backend, used only when
@env{CODEBUD_BACKEND} is @code{openai} (install the optional client with
@code{pip install "codebud[openai]"}).  The model used is
@code{gpt-3.5-turbo} unless overridden by @env{OPENAI_MODEL}.

@item OPENAI_MODEL
The OpenAI model to use with the @code{openai} backend.
Default: @code{gpt-3.5-turbo}.

@item CODEBUD_RAG
Set to @code{0} to stop adding project context from the index to the
planning prompt.  Default: @code{1} (used whenever the project has an
index).  @xref{The index Subcommand}.

@item CODEBUD_RAG_HINTS
Set to @code{0} to stop @command{chat} from suggesting @code{/index}.
Default: @code{1}.

@item WEB_SEARCH_ENABLED
Set to @code{1} to enable outbound DuckDuckGo web searches.
Default: @code{0} (disabled).  When disabled, the @code{web_search}
tool reports itself as unavailable to the planner.

@item XDG_DATA_HOME
@itemx XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where Codebud keeps its runtime data (@file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/codebud},
default @file{~/.local/share/codebud}) and your own policy files
(@file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/codebud}, default @file{~/.config/codebud})
(@pxref{Files and Directories}).
@end table

The @command{config} subcommand prints the resolved values of these
variables; the XDG directories it shows as the paths in use
(@pxref{The config Subcommand}).


@node Backwards Compatibility
@section Backwards Compatibility
@cindex backwards compatibility

Before subcommands were introduced, Codebud was invoked as:

@example
python run_agent.py "your request"
@end example

The script was later folded into the package, so that form no longer
exists; @code{python -m codebud} replaces it.  A request without a
subcommand still maps to @command{run}:

@example
codebud "your request"              @r{(maps to @command{run})}
codebud run "your request"          @r{(equivalent)}
python -m codebud "your request"    @r{(replaces python run_agent.py)}
@end example

The old interface's step-by-step approval flow is available as
@option{--interactive}/@option{-i} on @command{run}
(@pxref{The run Subcommand}), and again --- across multiple turns ---
in the @command{chat} subcommand's REPL.
